@node Overview, Installation, Top, Top
@comment  node-name,  next,  previous,  up
@chapter Overview

Zebra is free routing software that manages TCP/IP based routing
protocols.  IPv6 routing protocols are also supported.  Zebra is
distributed under the GNU General Public License

  You can get the latest information from the Zebra web page:
@url{http://www.zebra.org/}.

@menu
* What Zebra Is::               What is Zebra?
* System Architecture::         Zebra system architecture
* Supported Platforms::         Supported Platforms
* Mailing List::                Mailing list information.
* Bug Reports::                 
@end menu

@node What Zebra Is, System Architecture, Overview, Overview
@comment  node-name,  next,  previous,  up
@section What Zebra Is

Today, TCP/IP networks are covering all of the world. The Internet has
been deployed in many countries, companies, and at home. If you connect
to the Internet you use TCP/IP routing functions. Zebra is a free TCP/IP
routing software package. A Zebra installed box works as a dedicated
router. With Zebra your machine talks with other routers by means of
routing protocols and updates the kernel routing table. You can
dynamically change the configuration and view routing table information
from the Zebra terminal interface.

For a small network or a stub network there is no need for routing
software. The only thing you have to do is to set up the interfaces and
put a few commands about static routes and/or default routes. But if the
network is rather large, or if the network structure changes frequently,
you will need dynamic routing protocols such as RIP, OSPF or BGP. Zebra
is for you

Below is the list of currently supported routing protocol RFCs.

@table @samp
@item RFC1771
A Border Gateway Protocol 4 (BGP-4). Y. Rekhter & T. Li. March 1995.
@item RFC1966
BGP Route Reflection An alternative to full mesh IBGP. T. Bates &
R. Chandrasekeran. June 1996.
@item RFC1997
BGP Communities Attribute. R. Chandra, P. Traina & T. Li. August 1996.
@item RFC2080
RIPng for IPv6. G. Malkin, R. Minnear. January 1997.
@item RFC2283
Multiprotocol Extensions for BGP-4. T. Bates, R. Chandra, D.  Katz,
Y. Rekhter. February 1998.
@item RFC2328
OSPF Version 2. J. Moy. April 1998.
@item RFC2453
RIP Version 2. G. Malkin. November 1998.
@item RFC2545
Use of BGP-4 Multiprotocol Extensions for IPv6 Inter-Domain Routing. 
P. Marques, F. Dupont. March 1999.
@end table

@page
@node System Architecture, Supported Platforms, What Zebra Is, Overview
@comment  node-name,  next,  previous,  up
@section System Architecture

Traditional routing software are made as a one process program which
provides all of the routing protocol functionalities as a whole.  Zebra
takes a different approach, it is made from a collection of some daemons.
There are some protocol specific daemons and a kernel routing table
manager.

@code{ripd} handles the RIP protocol. @code{ospfd} is a daemon which
supports OSPF version 2.  @code{bgpd} supports the BGP-4 protocol.  For
changing the kernel routing table and for redistribution between
different routing protocols, there is @code{zebra} daemon.  There is no
need for these daemons to be running over the same machine.  This
architecture creates new possibilities for the routing system.  It is pretty
easy adding new routing protocol daemons to the system.

@example
@group
+----+  +----+  +-----+  +-----+
|bgpd|  |ripd|  |ospfd|  |zebra|
+----+  +----+  +-----+  +-----+
                            |
+---------------------------|--+
|                           v  |
|  UNIX Kernel  routing table  |
|                              |
+------------------------------+

    Zebra System Architecture
@end group
@end example

Zebra use a multi-threaded mechanism when it runs with a kernel that
supports multi-threads.  But at this moment the thread library which
comes with GNU/Linux or FreeBSD has some problems for running Zebra, so
we don't use threads on those OSes, instead we use the select system
call for multiplexing.

When zebra runs under a GNU Hurd kernel it will act as a kernel routing
table itself.  Under GNU Hurd all TCP/IP services are provided by user
processes called @code{pfinet}.  Zebra will provide all the routing
selection mechanisms for the process.  This feature will be implemented
in the future.

