@node Mailing List, Bug Reports, Supported Platforms, Overview
@comment  node-name,  next,  previous,  up
@section Mailing List

There is a mailing list for discussions about Zebra.  If you have any
comments or suggestions to Zebra please send to
@email{zebra@@zebra.org}.  New snapshot announcements, improvement
notes, and some patches are sent to the list.

To subscribe @email{zebra@@zebra.org, Zebra mailing list}, please send a
mail to @*@email{majordomo@@zebra.org} with a message body that includes
only

@quotation
subscribe zebra
@end quotation

To unsubscribe from the list, please send a mail to
@email{majordomo@@zebra.org} with a message body that includes only

@quotation
unsubscribe zebra
@end quotation

@node Bug Reports,  , Mailing List, Overview
@comment  node-name,  next,  previous,  up
@section Bug Reports

If you think you find a bug, please send a bug report to
@email{bug-zebra@@gnu.org}.  Zebra maintainers subscribe to the list.
When you send a bug report, please be careful about the points below.

@itemize @bullet
@item 
Please note what kind of OS you are using.  If you use the IPv6 stack
please note that.
@item
Please show us the results of @code{netstat -rn} and @code{ifconfig -a}.
Information from zebra's VTY command @code{show ip route} will also be
helpful.
@item
Please send your configuration file with the report.  If you specify the
argument to the configure script please note that too.
@end itemize

Bug reports are very important for us to improve the quality of Zebra.
Zebra is still in the development stage, but please don't hesitate to
send a bug report to @email{bug-zebra@@gnu.org}.


@node  Installation, Configuration File, Overview, Top
@comment  node-name,  next,  previous,  up
@chapter Installation

Zebra is in the development stage, so there is no earlier official
release.  Zebra's beta package can be found at
@url{ftp://ftp.zebra.org/pub/zebra/}.  Once it is officially released it
will be found at GNU FTP archive sites.

Zebra installation is very easy because of an excellent configure
script.  It automatically detects current states of the machine so
please check on kernel configurations before running configure scripts.

@menu
* GNU/Linux Configuration::     
* Configure Options::           
* VTY Port Setup::              
@end menu

@node GNU/Linux Configuration, Configure Options, Installation, Installation
@comment  node-name,  next,  previous,  up
@section GNU/Linux Configuration

GNU/Linux has very flexible kernel configuration features.  If you use
GNU/Linux, make it sure that the current kernel configuration is what
you want.  Zebra will run with any kernel configuration but some
recommendations do exist.

@table @var

@item CONFIG_NETLINK
Kernel/User netlink socket.  This is a brand new feature which enables
an advanced interface between the Linux kernel and Zebra (@pxref{Linux
Netlink}).

@item CONFIG_RTNETLINK
Routing messages.  This makes it possible to receive netlink routing
messages.  If you specify this option, @code{zebra} can detect routing
information updates directly from the kernel (@pxref{Kernel Interface}).

@item CONFIG_IP_MULTICAST
IP: multicasting.  This option should be specified when you use
@code{ripd} or @code{ospfd} because these protocols use multicast.

@end table

IPv6 supported is added from GNU/Linux kernel version 2.2.  If you try
to use the Zebra IPv6 feature on a GNU/Linux kernel, please make sure
the below library has been installed.

@table @code

@item inet6-apps
@code{inet6-apps} package includes basic IPv6 related libraries such as
@code{inet_ntop} and @code{inet_pton}.  Some basic IPv6 programs such as
@code{ping}, @code{ftp}, @code{inetd} are also
included. @code{inet-apps} can be found at
@url{ftp://ftp.inner.net/pub/ipv6/}.

@item net-tools
@code{net-tools} provides an IPv6 enabled interface and routing utility.
It contains @code{ifconfig}, @code{route}, @code{netstat} and other
tools.  @code{net-tools} can be found at @url{ftp://somewhere/}.

@end table

@node Configure Options, VTY Port Setup, GNU/Linux Configuration, Installation
@comment  node-name,  next,  previous,  up
@section Configure Options

Normally the Zebra configure scripts detect host configurations
automatically.  For manual configuration, there are several configure
options.  You can turn off IPv6 support or disable the making of some
daemons using configure options.

@table @code
@item --enable-guile
Turn on compilation of the zebra-guile interpreter.  You will need the
guile library to make this.
@item --disable-ipv6
Turn off IPv6 related features and daemons.
@item --disable-zebra
Do not build zebra daemon.
@item --disable-bgpd
Do not build bgpd.
@item --disable-ripd
Do not build ripd.
@item --disable-ripngd
Do not build ripngd.
@item --disable-ospfd
Do not build ospfd.
@item --disable-ospf6d
Do not build ospf6d.
@item --disable-pthread
Do not use pthread features.  Default is to disable this feature so you
don't need to specify it.
@end table

You can specify any of the above options to the configure script.
Configuration and compilation is very simple.  You run @code{configure}
and then @code{make} Zebra:

@example
% ./configure --disable-ipv6
% make
@end example

will create zebra and other beasts.  Installation is done by:

@example
% make install
@end example

This will install all of the Zebra software under @var{prefix}/sbin.
Sample configuration installed under @var{prefix}/etc.  If you want to
change the installation directory please specify the following options
to the configure script.

@table @code
@item --prefix=@var{prefix}
Install architecture-independent files in @var{prefix} [/usr/local].
@item --sysconfdir=@var{dir}
Read-only sample configuration file in @var{dir} [@var{prefix}/etc].
@end table

@node VTY Port Setup,  , Configure Options, Installation
@comment  node-name,  next,  previous,  up
@section VTY Port Setup

Zebra daemon's have their own terminal interfaces each is called a VTY.
So after the Zebra beasts are installed you have to setup each program's
port number for connecting to them.  Please add the following entries to
@file{/etc/services}.

@example
zebrasrv      2600/tcp		  # zebra service
zebra         2601/tcp		  # zebra vty
ripd          2602/tcp		  # RIPd vty
ripngd        2603/tcp		  # RIPngd vty
ospfd         2604/tcp		  # OSPFd vty
bgpd          2605/tcp		  # BGPd vty
ospf6d        2606/tcp		  # OSPF6d vty
@end example

If you use a FreeBSD newer than 2.2.8, the above entry is already added
to @file{/etc/services}.  So there is no need to add it.

@node Configuration File, VTY, Installation, Top
@comment  node-name,  next,  previous,  up
@chapter Configuration File

Each of the Zebra beasts has its own configuration file.  For example,
zebra's default configuration file name is @file{zebra.conf}.  Program
name plus @file{.conf} is the default configuration file name. You can
specify a configuration file by -f or --config-file option to the
daemon.

In a configuration file, you can write the debugging options, a vty's
password, routing daemon configurations, a logfile name, and so on.

@menu
* Basic Configuration::         
* Sample Configuration::        
@end menu

@node Basic Configuration, Sample Configuration, Configuration File, Configuration File
@comment  node-name,  next,  previous,  up
@section Basic Configuration

@deffn Command {hostname @var{hostname}} {}
Set hostname of the router.
@end deffn

@deffn Command {password @var{password}} {}
Set password for vty interface.  If there is no password, a vty won't
accept connections.
@end deffn

@deffn Command {enable password @var{password}} {}
Set enable password.
@end deffn

@deffn Command {log stdout} {}
@deffnx Command {no log stdout} {}
Set loggin output to stdout.
@end deffn

@deffn Command {log file @var{filename}} {}
If you want to log into a file please specify @code{filename} as
follows.
@example
log file /usr/local/etc/bgpd.log
@end example
@end deffn

@deffn Command {log syslog} {}
@deffnx Command {no log syslog} {}
Set loggin output to syslog.
@end deffn

@deffn Command {write terminal} {}
Displays the current configuration to the vty interface.
@end deffn

@deffn Command {write file} {}
Write current configuration to configuration file.
@end deffn

@deffn Command {configure terminal} {}
Change to configuration mode.  This command is the first step to
configuration.
@end deffn

@deffn Command {terminal length @var{<0-512>}} {}
Set terminal display length to @var{<0-512>}.  If length is 0, no
display control is performed.
@end deffn

@deffn Command {who} {}
@end deffn

@deffn Command {list} {}
List commands.
@end deffn

@deffn Command {service password-encryption} {}
Encrypt password.
@end deffn

@deffn Command {service advanced-vty} {}
Enable advanced mode VTY.
@end deffn

@deffn Command {service terminal-length @var{<0-512>}} {}
Set system wide line configuration.  This configuration command applies
to all VTY interfaces.
@end deffn

@deffn Command {show version} {}
Show the current version of the Zebra and it's build host information.
@end deffn

@deffn Command {line vty} {}
Enter vty configuration mode.
@end deffn

@deffn Command {banner motd default} {}
Set default motd string.
@end deffn

@deffn Command {no banner motd} {}
No motd banner string will be printed.
@end deffn

@deffn {Line Command} {exec-timeout @var{second}} {}
Set time out in seconds.
@end deffn

@deffn {Line Command} {access-class @var{access-list}} {}
Restrict vty connections with an access list.
@end deffn

@node   Sample Configuration,  , Basic Configuration, Configuration File
@comment  node-name,  next,  previous,  up
@section Sample Configuration

Below is sample configuration of the zebra daemon.

@example
@group
!
! Zebra configuration file
!
hostname Router
password zebra
enable password zebra
!
log stdout
@end group
@end example

'!' and '#' are comment characters.  If the first character of the word
is one of comment characters then, from the rest of the line will be
ignored as a comment.

@example
password zebra!password
@end example

If a comment character is not the first character of the word, it's a
normal character. So above '!' will not be regarded as a comment and the
password is set to 'zebra!password'.


